--TEST--
`new` on a `Core` class is held to the constructor roster, and an unregistered `Core` name is undeclared rather than a failure below the checker — `rule:types/declaration`, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A `Core` class is not constructed by a `constructor` member: its instances
// come from the member that produces one, and the whole roster of names `new`
// may be written on is `nvs_stdlib::registry::CONSTRUCTORS` — spec § 9's
// collections and nothing else. So the two mistakes below are different ones,
// and neither is the arity count a user class gets.

// The accepted half, written first so that a name that stopped being
// constructible shows up here rather than as a missing refusal below.
Core\ObjectSet<int> $set = new Core\ObjectSet<int>();
$set->add(1);
echo $set->count(), "\n";

// Registered, but with no constructor: the refusal names the member the class
// does not have, and says the same thing however many arguments are written.
mixed $bare = new Core\Str();
mixed $args = new Core\Str("a", 1);

// Not registered at all: `Core\` is a spelling, not a declaration, so this is
// the ordinary undeclared-class answer rather than a failure below the
// checker.
mixed $gone = new Core\Bogus();
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Str` has no member named `constructor`
   --> %s:16:15
   |
16 | mixed $bare = new Core\Str();
   |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ referenced here

error[E0405]: `Core\Str` has no member named `constructor`
   --> %s:17:15
   |
17 | mixed $args = new Core\Str("a", 1);
   |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ referenced here

error[E0303]: `Core\Bogus` is not declared
   --> %s:22:19
   |
22 | mixed $gone = new Core\Bogus();
   |                   ^^^^^^^^^^ no matching declaration

error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
